WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:04.033
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy Award-winning actress

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:05.167
and director you know from

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.067
the hit comedy
"Parks and Recreation,"

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:09.434
the films "Mean Girls"
and "Wine Country,"

00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:11.334
and, of course, her work
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:13.634
She directed the new
documentary, "Lucy and Desi,"

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:15.801
which premiers
tomorrow on Amazon Prime Video.

00:00:15.801 --> 00:00:17.834
Please welcome back to the show
one of the best,

00:00:17.834 --> 00:00:19.801
our very good friend
Amy Poehler, everybody!

00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:21.801
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:39.267
Hello!
-Hi!

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:42.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
Hello, hello!

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:44.267
Oh, it's nice to be back.

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:47.367
-It is so nice to be back.
-And an audience, too.

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:48.734
-I know. Last time --

00:00:48.734 --> 00:00:50.567
-Last time,
there was no audience.

00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:52.167
-You did a few for us
with no audiences.

00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:54.234
-Boy, it's really nice to see
those faces out there.

00:00:54.234 --> 00:00:55.400
-It is good to see
human beings again.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.601
-It really is.
-Yeah.

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:58.901
-I love human beings.
-Yeah, you've always.

00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:00.834
That's one of the first things
we connected on.

00:01:00.834 --> 00:01:03.100
-That's right.
We're like, "I love humans.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.267
You love humans, too!"

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:05.267
-Remember, I was like,

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:06.567
"I can't believe it!"

00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:08.601
A couple of anniversaries
just happened.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:11.334
I can't believe it was
seven years since

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.868
"Parks" had its finale,

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:14.400
which is crazy
that that's seven years ago.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.400
-Yeah.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.334
-Eight years ago
since this show started,

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:18.767
and you were
our very first guest.

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:20.133
-I know. It was really exciting.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.567
-Yes.
-I know.

00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:24.234
And you had another guest,
and one of us became president.

00:01:24.234 --> 00:01:26.234
-Yes.
-And it wasn't me.

00:01:26.234 --> 00:01:27.634
-I always say --
I'm going to say it

00:01:27.634 --> 00:01:28.934
every time you're here.

00:01:28.934 --> 00:01:30.701
Guests on my first show
were you and Joe Biden.

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:31.834
-Yeah.

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:34.534
-And Joe Biden, we said,
"The only thing is,

00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:35.834
I really want to
have Amy first."

00:01:35.834 --> 00:01:37.367
And he was like,
"That sounds good to me."

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.701
And I got to be honest,
I think that speaks to a man

00:01:39.701 --> 00:01:42.634
who has the first
female vice president,

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:44.567
putting the first black woman
on the Supreme Court,

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:46.634
and he let Amy Poehler
go first here.

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:48.434
-That's right.
Joe knows what I like.

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:51.267
-Joe knows what you like.
-Joe knows what I like.

00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.300
I have a present
for you, though.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.300
-Do you really?

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.400
-Yeah, because, you know,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.868
the eighth anniversary
of something --

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.367
You know, you have to give,
like, paper or wood.

00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.567
-Yeah.
-And eight is bronze.

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.734
-Okay. Is it really bronze
or are you making it up?

00:02:03.734 --> 00:02:05.934
-I'm not. It's really bronze.
I looked it up, Seth.

00:02:05.934 --> 00:02:07.801
-Okay.

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:11.067
-And so I got you a bronze
medal for all of your work.

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:12.133
-Did you really?

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.400
That's beautiful.
-Isn't that beautiful?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.501
-It is.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.367
-So just know that
you've been working hard

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.434
for eight years,
and you got the bronze.

00:02:19.434 --> 00:02:21.267
-The bronze.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:22.601 --> 00:02:24.234
It's beautiful.
-Yeah.

00:02:24.234 --> 00:02:25.467
-I love this.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.734
-You know,
there's a study that says

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.334
that bronze medalists
are the happiest.

00:02:29.334 --> 00:02:30.667
-Really?

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.467
-Yes, because gold medalists
have a lot of pressure,

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:33.834
and they think
they're going to win.

00:02:33.834 --> 00:02:35.834
Silver medalists
are mad they didn't win.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:38.200
And bronze people are like,
"I can't believe I'm up here."

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.434
-Yeah! That makes sense.

00:02:40.434 --> 00:02:41.934
And then the rest of
your life, I feel like

00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:43.167
if you're the silver,

00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:45.234
if you tell someone you're
a silver medallist,

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:46.234
people will always say,
"How close were you?"

00:02:46.234 --> 00:02:47.567
-Exactly. Exactly.
-Yeah.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.834
-But bronze, it's like,
"I could have been eighth."

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:51.834
-Yeah. They're like,
"Do you know how bad

00:02:51.834 --> 00:02:52.868
this could have been, sucker?"

00:02:52.868 --> 00:02:54.968
-Yeah.
So, congrats on eight years.

00:02:54.968 --> 00:02:57.801
-Last time I saw you, it was a
deeply fun couple of days.

00:02:57.801 --> 00:02:59.200
We got to go to
Washington, D.C.,

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.601
because Lorne Michaels got
honored at the Kennedy Center.

00:03:01.601 --> 00:03:02.667
-Yeah.

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:04.033
-And what was
really fun about it --

00:03:04.033 --> 00:03:06.501
it was sort of
multi-generational "SNL" people.

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:08.067
We got to hang out backstage.

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:09.734
Do you have any other memories?

00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:11.000
-Nope.
-Okay.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:12.234
Let's just move on.

00:03:12.234 --> 00:03:14.801
-They're just kind of like
a wash of people, right?

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:17.868
It was like, there were stairs.
There was some kind of carpet.

00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:19.334
-Yeah.
-Joni Mitchell.

00:03:19.334 --> 00:03:21.067
-Yeah. Yeah. That's great.
-Smokey Robinson.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:24.634
-You should write a book
about this night.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:28.067
-I should just do a book
that's just names and carpets

00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:30.067
and -- It was so fun.

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:31.601
It was so fun to do it with you.
-Yeah.

00:03:31.601 --> 00:03:36.467
-And it was -- What a night.
Don't remember anything from it.

00:03:36.467 --> 00:03:39.701
Not because I was --
I wasn't drunk, none of that.

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:42.934
It's just now,
life is just like a watercolor.

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:44.133
-Oh, interesting.

00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:45.334
So you've moved into
your watercolor period.

00:03:45.334 --> 00:03:47.300
-I'm in more of,
like, my poetry phase,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.634
where I'm like,
a name, a flower, a scent.

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:51.000
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.767
-That's really interesting,
because you were always --

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:54.000
-It is interesting.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:01.367
So, well, you had
a celebration recently.

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:02.534
-Yes.

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:05.133
-I know there's this
very tight group of women

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:07.300
who worked at "SNL"
at the same time.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.400
You guys are on
a text chain

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.334
that you won't let me
look at or see.

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:12.501
-No. No boys allowed.
-No boys allowed.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:16.534
But you guys had a birthday
party for you and Tina and --

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:18.868
-Me and Tina and Emily Spivey,
a writer on our show --

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:20.567
we all celebrated our
50th birthday

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:22.234
during the pandemic,
as a lot of people did,

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:23.934
so we didn't really get
a chance to have a party.

00:04:23.934 --> 00:04:26.300
So we had a big bash together
just this week.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:27.634
-And you guys all --

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:29.100
You know, you and Tina
knew each other before,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.868
but you met Emily at "SNL."

00:04:30.868 --> 00:04:31.934
Somebody made you a cake.

00:04:31.934 --> 00:04:34.868
-Yeah, Rachel Dratch
made us a cake.

00:04:35.534 --> 00:04:38.601
Rachel Dratch made us that cake.

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:40.534
And she made us video,
too, that was --

00:04:40.534 --> 00:04:41.968
Oh, my God, did it make us bawl.

00:04:41.968 --> 00:04:42.968
We had the best time.

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:44.934
And we did this Zoom thing

00:04:44.934 --> 00:04:46.601
where we learned about
our enneagrams.

00:04:46.601 --> 00:04:47.667
Does anyone know
about enneagrams?

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:48.934
[ Light cheering ]

00:04:48.934 --> 00:04:50.300
-Okay, so more people
than I would have guessed.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:54.567
-So, okay, well, there's things
outside your world, Seth.

00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:57.934
But it's like a number that
is like --

00:04:57.934 --> 00:04:59.534
It's like a personality test.
-Uh-huh.

00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:01.534
-I made you take it.
-You did.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:02.667
-You are a...

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:03.667
-3.
-Yes.

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:05.000
-Yeah.
[ Light cheering ]

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.067
-You got a couple 3s.
-Got a couple 3s in the house.

00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:08.767
-And 3s,
from what I understand,

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:11.267
are driven,
ambitious, hard-working.

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:12.400
They're called "the achievers."

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.701
-Okay, I'll take it. Thank you.

00:05:13.701 --> 00:05:16.100
-They're the talk-show hosts.
-Yeah. Okay, gotcha.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.667
-And they work really hard,
and they're charming.

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:21.033
And we all
got -- We got our numbers

00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:22.133
and we all found out
our numbers

00:05:22.133 --> 00:05:23.767
and found out more
about our numbers.

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:27.067
-And, now, you believe --
If you could go back,

00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:28.801
would you have wanted
to know everybody's number

00:05:28.801 --> 00:05:30.601
before you started collaborating
with them at "SNL"?

00:05:30.601 --> 00:05:32.133
-I actually think
it was really interesting,

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:34.133
because it kind of, like,
tells you what motivates you.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:35.701
Like, 3s like a lot of praise,

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:38.067
like, "You're doing great
so far, Seth.

00:05:38.067 --> 00:05:40.501
This is a great show.
You got your medal."

00:05:40.501 --> 00:05:42.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:42.868 --> 00:05:45.234
-We like our medal.
We like our medal.

00:05:45.234 --> 00:05:47.267
-Yep, you got your medal.

00:05:47.267 --> 00:05:49.467
And I'm an 8,
which is "the challenger."

00:05:49.467 --> 00:05:51.667
-Whoo!
-8s!

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:53.667
So, I'm a challenger.

00:05:53.667 --> 00:05:55.234
Like, safety and security
is important to me.

00:05:55.234 --> 00:05:56.868
I want to know who is in charge.

00:05:56.868 --> 00:05:59.601
And are you the person
in charge here?

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:02.367
-I mean,
I feel like I am today.

00:06:02.367 --> 00:06:03.467
-Today. Okay, okay.
Good to know.

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:04.634
-Now, wait.

00:06:04.634 --> 00:06:06.534
Does this mean that
this is just branding,

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:07.901
the 8 on my medal?

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:08.901
-Oh, my God!

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:12.234
I should wear this
'cause I'm an 8.

00:06:12.234 --> 00:06:14.501
I didn't even think about that,
but, yes, it is about me.

00:06:14.501 --> 00:06:16.400
-That's the thing,
because 8s are very

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.467
passive-aggressive
with their gift giving.

00:06:18.467 --> 00:06:20.734
-Yes. They give you a gift,
and then you're like,

00:06:20.734 --> 00:06:21.834
"Wait a minute.

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:24.000
It might look better on me."

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.167
-Classic 8.
-Classic 8.

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:26.801
-That's why it's an 8,
because it's like --

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:29.000
-3s and 8s love each other.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.901
-Oh, no, I want to ask this.
Are we good?

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:32.901
Was it -- Would it make sense,
if you didn't know anything

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:34.634
about our styles,
if you were putting together

00:06:34.634 --> 00:06:37.000
an "Update" duo, to say,
"Put a 3 and 8"?

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.534
-100%. Here's why.

00:06:38.534 --> 00:06:40.701
Because 3s work really hard
and they want to achieve,

00:06:40.701 --> 00:06:42.968
and 8s are like, "We got this."

00:06:42.968 --> 00:06:45.834
And 3s are like,
"We don't have this."

00:06:45.834 --> 00:06:48.567
I'm like, "We got this!"
-Oh, so, interesting.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:51.100
The thing that you want to know,
who's in charge,

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.801
because you want to then --

00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:55.667
Like, that means you are not,
then, in charge.

00:06:55.667 --> 00:06:56.868
-Let me be clear.

00:06:56.868 --> 00:06:58.701
I don't really know
what I'm talking about.

00:06:58.701 --> 00:07:00.734
-Yeah.

00:07:00.734 --> 00:07:02.701
-And I know there are people
that are much better

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:04.467
at explaining this,
but because I'm an 8,

00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:05.801
I am already an expert.

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:07.434
Like, I think I'm an expert.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:09.968
-But what I believe to be true
is 8s like 3s.

00:07:09.968 --> 00:07:12.701
They like hard work.
They like that. They like that.

00:07:12.701 --> 00:07:14.200
That's where
they get a lot of self-esteem.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.300
-Well, it sounds like
what I liked --

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.634
I mean, I could describe
it outside the number system,

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:22.834
but it seems like what I would
like about working with an 8 is

00:07:22.834 --> 00:07:25.567
somebody who --
I get very stressful

00:07:25.567 --> 00:07:28.701
about how things are going to
go, and doing "Update" with you,

00:07:28.701 --> 00:07:30.868
you did have a vibe of like,
"We got this,"

00:07:30.868 --> 00:07:33.934
which was exactly what I needed
before going onstage.

00:07:33.934 --> 00:07:36.501
-Yeah. I think -- You know,
it was so interesting

00:07:36.501 --> 00:07:38.534
to hear everybody's number
and also just to, like,

00:07:38.534 --> 00:07:39.868
figure out
what motivates people,

00:07:39.868 --> 00:07:41.701
like, how they like to work.

00:07:41.701 --> 00:07:44.434
And, you know,
you read some numbers

00:07:44.434 --> 00:07:48.033
and you're like,
"The emotional artist?

00:07:48.033 --> 00:07:51.567
Like, oh, my God!
How do you get through the day?

00:07:51.567 --> 00:07:53.501
Do you sit in
your feelings all day?

00:07:53.501 --> 00:07:56.767
That must be nice."

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:58.767
Those are 4s.

00:07:58.767 --> 00:08:01.467
No, but it was
really fun to do it

00:08:01.467 --> 00:08:02.501
and it was fun
to do it with the ladies,

00:08:02.501 --> 00:08:03.634
and we were
laughing really hard,

00:08:03.634 --> 00:08:06.100
'cause it was explaining
all of us to a "T."

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:07.701
-That -- I wish
I would be there,

00:08:07.701 --> 00:08:10.100
and one of these days,
you should allow boys.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:11.234
-No, no boys allowed.

00:08:11.234 --> 00:08:12.367
-Okay, great.
-Sorry.

00:08:12.367 --> 00:08:13.567
-I do have
a lot more to ask you.

00:08:13.567 --> 00:08:14.634
We'll be right back with more
from Amy Poehler.

00:08:14.634 --> 00:08:17.133
-I'll just put my medal on.

